WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.940
I love making art and looking at artworks.
我热爱创作艺术，也喜欢欣赏艺术作品。

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.700
I’ve found myself wondering how we gain pleasure from art.
我发现自己一直在思考：我们是如何从艺术中获得愉悦的？

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.090
And now neuroaesthetics, a combination of neuroscience and aesthetics, may provide an answer.
而如今，结合了神经科学与美学的神经美学，或许能给出答案。

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:21.320
Neuroaesthetics is a relatively young field of research on what happens in the brain when we make aesthetic assessments.
神经美学是一个相对年轻的研究领域，研究的是我们进行审美评判时大脑中发生了什么。

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:29.340
Researchers use brain imaging techniques to see which brain areas light up when we view paintings that we consider beautiful.
研究人员借助脑成像技术，观察我们观看自己认为美的画作时，大脑的哪些区域会亮起来。

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:40.830
Similar research has been done to understand the “neuronal fireworks” that occur when we look at inspiring sculptures, attractive faces, impressive dance, etc.
为了弄清我们观看鼓舞人心的雕塑、迷人的面孔、令人赞叹的舞蹈等时出现的“神经元烟花”，人们也开展了类似的研究。

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:44.690
But why do we find some art beautiful and other art ugly?
但是，为什么我们会觉得有些艺术美、有些艺术丑呢？

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:49.210
According to research, it all comes down to the “aesthetic triad”.
据研究，这一切都归结于“审美三元组合”。

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.510
The first part of the triad is sensory-motor.
三元组合的第一部分是感觉—运动。

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.030
This involves perceiving things like colours, shapes and movements.
这涉及对颜色、形状和动作等事物的感知。

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.150
Movement in art has an interesting role.
艺术中的动作所起的作用很有意思。

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:09.470
If you see a painting of a movement, like of a man pulling his arm away after being bitten by a dog, you feel as if you are going through a similar experience.
如果你看到一幅描绘动作的画，比如一个男人被狗咬后把手臂缩回去，你会感觉自己仿佛也在经历类似的体验。

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:14.250
The part of your brain that controls your own movements lights up in response.
你大脑中控制自身动作的那部分会随之亮起来。

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.870
Second is emotion-valuation.
第二部分是情绪—评价。

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.670
This is how a piece of art makes you feel, and whether or not you appreciate or enjoy that feeling.
这指的是一件艺术作品给你带来怎样的感受，以及你是否欣赏或享受那种感受。

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:28.980
The part of the brain related to pleasure is activated in response to something we find beautiful.
一遇到我们觉得美的事物，大脑中与愉悦相关的部分就会被激活。

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:37.870
This system can be affected in fascinating ways, as found by research using transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS).
使用经颅磁刺激（TMS）的研究发现，这一系统可能会受到一些奇妙的影响。

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:48.030
If TMS is applied to a specific part of your brain behind your forehead that is particularly important for decision-making, you suddenly like different kinds of art.
如果对你前额后方一个对决策尤为重要的特定大脑区域施加TMS，你喜欢的艺术类型会突然改变。

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:55.520
Such stimulation produces significant changes in aesthetic appreciation of faces, bodies and artworks.
这种刺激会使人们对面孔、身体和艺术作品的审美欣赏发生显著变化。

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.060
The third part is meaning-knowledge.
第三部分是意义—知识。

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.850
This is to do with how we can connect with a piece of art and what meaning we can create in it.
这关系到我们如何与一件艺术作品建立联系，以及我们能在其中创造出什么意义。

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:12.510
Art is deeply personal, because when two people see the same artwork, their perceptions can create vastly different experiences of meaning.
艺术极具个人色彩，因为两个人看同一件艺术作品时，他们各自的感知可能带来截然不同的意义体验。

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.200
If we find meaning, then we often find pleasure.
如果我们找到了意义，往往也就能获得愉悦。

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.260
We also get enjoyment from the knowledge of how something was made.
了解某样东西是如何制作出来的，也会给我们带来乐趣。

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:28.480
For the images that an artist creates, viewers will probably get far more enjoyment once they know the process used to create them.
就艺术家创作的画面而言，观众一旦了解了创作这些画面的过程，很可能会获得多得多的乐趣。

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:41.630
Informed by neuroaesthetics, the next time I create my art I will value the process even more, enjoying the activation of the aesthetic triad in my brain as I admire the vivid images that I have created.
了解了神经美学之后，下次进行艺术创作时，我会更加珍视创作过程，一边欣赏自己创作出的生动画面，一边享受大脑中审美三元组合被激活的感觉。
